Z||000010||Will||02-15-2001||10:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sluppy123:<BR><B>I program a CNC machining center where I work, I can machine just about anything you can think of. A few weeks ago I was bored and made these, I plan to stick them on the under side of the deck lid. I could do the same thing with a ashtray door, If anyone has a extra pair laying around I could give it a shot.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>But you'd have to use the Fiero font!||||reg||1
Z||000011||ThatRickGuy||02-15-2001||01:40 PM||||I didn't know CNC machines came with fonts now. The last one I worked with you had to do everything w/ 3 dimentional points and plot along, not too bad until you get into circles, then, well, the more points you put in, the more rounded the circle.||||reg||1
Z||000012||sluppy123||02-15-2001||05:58 PM||||Most CNC machines use G code programing, there are a few out there that use a user-interface where a programer plays fill in the blank with the machine(Bridgeport EZ path's use this). <P>The machines I program use G code. The less complex parts are programed by hand using G code(GO,G1,G2 ect), & the very complex parts are programed using a CAM system. A CAD generated drawing is Fed into a CAM program which spits out a program that can be sent to the CNC machine. <P>I don't think there's a font out there that couldn't be cut. Most New machines have Pre-programmed functions(Engraving, Pocket milling....) The user defines the Text, Size,& font and the machine programs it.||||reg||1
